21:01:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you — and I didn’t mean to imply you pulled the idea out of thin air. If every developer you’ve personally worked with or dated has struggled with client-facing soft skills, it makes complete sense that you’d want to avoid repeating those patterns, especially on a project you care about. That’s not prejudice; it’s pattern recognition based on your real history. So I’d never ask you to ignore your experience. And yet, the question you’re facing now is about *this specific person*, not a statistical average. The risk of acting on the assumption that he’ll be awkward or bad at client small talk is that you might inadvertently create exactly the bottleneck you’re afraid of — or you might miss out on a partner who actually thrives with a different style of client interaction than you’ve seen before. So maybe a practical middle ground: **test rather than assume.** You don’t have to hand over the keys to all client comms on day one. Start with a low-stakes call where you’re both present, and you can steer if needed. If he handles it well, you’ve just added a huge asset to the client relationship (and freed up your own plate). If he’s exactly like your past experiences, you have observed evidence to frame a conversation like: *“Hey, let’s play to our strengths — I’ll handle much of the client side, and you focus on the build. When technical details come up, I’ll pull you in.”* That way you’re not denying your real-world patterns, you’re just giving this partnership the benefit of the doubt, with a safety net. And if he *does* surprise you, that’s a win for everyone.
